shortAnswer: "An embedding turns a piece of text into a list of numbers that represents its meaning. Text about similar things produces similar numbers, so a system can find related material even when the wording is completely different. It's what lets an AI search your documents for 'staff leave' and return a policy that only ever says 'annual holiday entitlement'."
faq:
  - q: "Do I need to understand embeddings to use AI?"
    a: "No. It's under-the-bonnet machinery. It's worth knowing the concept because it explains why AI document search finds things keyword search misses — and why it occasionally returns something that feels related but isn't what you wanted."
  - q: "How is this different from a normal search?"
    a: "Keyword search matches characters — 'invoice' finds documents containing 'invoice'. Embedding search matches meaning, so it also finds 'bill', 'statement' and 'amount payable'. It's more useful most of the time, and less literal when you wanted literal."
  - q: "Is this the same as RAG?"
    a: "Embeddings are a component of RAG rather than the whole thing. RAG is the pattern of retrieving relevant material and giving it to the model; embeddings are usually how the relevant material gets found."
  - q: "Where are the embeddings stored?"
    a: "In a vector database, which is built to compare these number lists quickly. For most businesses this is entirely hidden inside whichever product you're using — you never touch it directly."
  - q: "Does making embeddings send our documents somewhere?"
    a: "Usually yes — the text is sent to a provider to be converted. So the same data questions apply as any other AI processing: which tier, which provider, and whether the material should be leaving your systems at all."
